{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When there is found in your midst, in one of your gates that YHWH your Elohim gives you</b> — I have already explained (Ramban on Deuteronomy 11:32) that whenever Scripture speaks of statutes, it first mentions the law of idolatry or its worshippers. And it says \"in one of your gates that YHWH your Elohim\" etc., but this does not mean that it is only in the land of Israel, for even one who worships outside the land is liable to stoning. Rather, the meaning of the verse is: when the matter is found in one of the distant cities that HaShem will give you as He expands your border, and it is told to you and you hear about it — in whatever city you happen to be — you shall investigate the matter thoroughly, and when you know that it is true, you shall bring them out to the gate of that city where they worshipped and stone them. And it says (Deuteronomy 17:4) \"this abomination has been done in Israel,\" to make liable even outside the land, for concerning the wayward city it says (Deuteronomy 13:15) \"this abomination has been done in your midst,\" but here it mentions <b>in one of your gates</b>, speaking of the present situation as we have explained, and afterward it mentions <b>in Israel</b>, to teach that this applies to all Israel. And further, it was necessary to say <b>in one of your gates</b>, to teach that he is stoned at the gate where he worshipped, and this is in the land, for outside the land he is stoned at the entrance of the court that judged him. And this commandment is elucidated, for it already said (Exodus 22:19) \"one who slaughters to elohim shall be banned,\" and that applies in every place and at every time; therefore it repeats it here to explain what needs to be added to the commandment and to be brief regarding what has already been mentioned.\n\nAnd by way of truth, it is possible that it mentioned <b>in one of your gates</b> because it says <b>to transgress His covenant</b>, and that is the abomination done in Israel — Scripture informs of the covenant in the land of the covenant, but one who dwells outside the land is as though he worships idolatry. And I have already mentioned this matter (Leviticus 18:25).\n\nAnd it mentions \"a man or a woman,\" for because of the lightheadedness of the woman she may be drawn to worship idolatry through a sign or wonder performed before her, and the proof is the women of Jeremiah (Jeremiah 44). And likewise it mentions concerning the ov and the yide'oni (Leviticus 20:27) <b>or a woman</b>, because of the women's habitual involvement with them, as it says (Exodus 22:17) \"a sorceress you shall not let live.\" And in the Sifrei (Shofetim 148) they darshened that because regarding the seducer it says (Deuteronomy 13:14) \"worthless men have gone out,\" and that law is not so regarding women, it was necessary to specify concerning the worshipper that it applies equally to man and woman.\n\nAnd the reason for \"which I did not command\" is explicit."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:15.617384244Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}